<script>
	import Statement from '$lib/components/Statement.svelte';
	import Proof from '$lib/components/Proof.svelte';
	import * as mode from './statements/mode.md';
	import * as changeOfMeasure from './statements/change-of-measure.md';
</script>

Once quantities are observed, a [model built forward](../generative-models/) describes the free
ones by their posterior, the conditional law of
[%full](statement:math/generative-models/conditioning). The posterior is a whole law. Its
expectations are integrals over every free quantity at once, and have a closed form for almost no
model. What can always be asked of it is where its density is greatest. That point is the estimate
this part of the mathematics computes, and the Gaussian approximations later in the part are
fitted around it. This note defines it, says why it is the first thing to compute, and shows the
one property of it that is easy to forget: it depends on the measure its density is taken against.

## The objective and its minimum

<Statement {...mode} />

The objective is the negative logarithm of the posterior's density, up to a constant. By
[%full](statement:math/generative-models/conditioning) the posterior's density against $\nu_F$ is
$\density(\point_F, \point_O) / \density_O(\point_O)$, and the denominator does not depend on
$\point_F$. So

$$
	-\log \density_{F \mid O}(\point_F \mid \point_O) = f(\point_F) + \log \density_O(\point_O) , @tag(posterior)
$$

and the mode is found without the normalizer $\density_O$, an integral over every free quantity
that is as hard to compute as the expectations themselves. That is why the mode comes first: it
needs the joint density alone, evaluated at points, and its derivatives.

A model with parameters describes the measure [](eq:math/generative-models/parameter), whose
density is $S(\theta)\, \density_\theta(\point_F, \point_O)$. Its objective is the negative
logarithm of that, $-\log S(\theta) - \log \density_\theta(\point_F, \point_O)$, and its minimizer
is a maximum a posteriori estimate when the measure is a posterior and a maximum likelihood
estimate of $\theta$ when $S \equiv 1$. The two are the same computation, and nothing below
distinguishes them.

A mode need not exist, nor be unique. A density that grows without bound toward the edge of its
space has no mode, a symmetric one may have several, and a posterior that is not concentrated has
modes that say little about where its mass is. The notes that follow find local modes, points
where the [chart gradient](../derivatives-in-charts/) of $f$ vanishes and its curvature is
positive, and say what can be read off one.

## A mode depends on the measure

A density is a density against a reference measure, and so is its mode.

<Statement {...changeOfMeasure} />

<Proof>

For measurable $A$, $\int_A (\density_{F \mid O} / h)\, \dd \nu_F' = \int_A \density_{F \mid O}\, \dd
\nu_F$, so $\density_{F \mid O} / h$ is the posterior's density against $\nu_F'$, and taking the
negative logarithm of the joint density divided by $h$ gives $f'$.

</Proof>

The posterior does not move: the same law, the same expectations, the same credible sets. Only
its density changes, and with it the point at which the density is greatest. The mode is a
property of the pair of a law and a reference measure, and not of the law alone.

**The positive reals.** Let $\randomPoint$ be log-normal, $\log \randomPoint \sim \mathcal N(m,
s^2)$. Against Lebesgue measure its density is $\frac{1}{\point} \mathcal N(\log \point;\, m, s^2)$,
and its objective $\log \point + (\log \point - m)^2 / 2 s^2$, up to a constant, is least at
$\point = e^{m - s^2}$. Against the measure $\dd \point / \point$, $h(\point) = 1/\point$, the
objective loses the $\log \point$, and the mode is $e^m$, the median. Both are the mode of the same
law. At $s = 1$ they differ by a factor of $e$.

**In a chart.** The [chart at a point](../chart-volume/) $\mu$ carries the reference measure to a
measure on its coordinates, and by [](eq:math/chart-volume/chart-integral) the density of the
coordinate $\tangent$ of $\mu \oplus \tangent$ against Lebesgue measure is the density of the
point times $e^{\lambda_\mu(\tangent)}$. Its objective is the chart objective

$$
	f(\mu \oplus \tangent) - \lambda_\mu(\tangent) , @tag(chart-objective)
$$

whose minimizer is the image of a mode of $f$ only where the log-volume $\lambda_\mu$ is constant
in $\tangent$ or its gradient vanishes there. On a Euclidean space it is zero. On a curved space it
is not, and a mode read in a chart's coordinates is not the mode against the space's measure. A
method that works in charts, as every method of this part does, says which of the two it computes:
the steps of the next notes minimize $f$ and read its derivatives in charts, so they find a mode
against the space's own measure, and a Gaussian fitted in a chart is fitted to
[](eq:chart-objective).

## The objective is a sum of terms

The joint density of a model built forward is a product of its kernels' densities, its
likelihood factors and its scores, so its objective is a sum,

$$
	f(\point_F) = \sum_t \phi_t(\point_F) , @tag(sum)
$$

one *term* $\phi_t$ for each kernel, factor and score, as the
[logarithms of a model](../generative-models/#working-with-a-model-built-forward) are. Each term
reads only the few quantities its kernel reads, its *variables* $K_t$, and the observed quantities
are held at their values. So the gradient of $f$ is a sum of gradients that are each zero outside
the coordinates of a few quantities, and its Hessian a sum of blocks, each on the variables of one
term. Two quantities that share no term have a zero block between them. The methods that find a
mode inherit this structure, and the factorization that ends this part's linear algebra is built
around it.
